data: {"id":"chatcmpl_6235d011-89c7-48ed-9fd6-f9d5ba2a93f0","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1792436456,"model":"oracle/chrono-chatgpt-pro-pool","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"ACCEPTI find no proof error in Lemmas 1–3 of    low-charge-rank-barrier.md, using the incidence-rank dependency supplied in your request and the note’s stated hypotheses. The quadratic capacity bound is correct, the necessary residual-charge threshold is four, and the charge-four residual incidence graph is completely classified.The final search paragraph needs a careful reading: its original-charge-four and “no old corner donors” restrictions are legitimate choices for a restricted first search. They are not consequences for every residual-charge-four configuration. Because the paragraph says “can require” and explicitly distinguishes a later reservation-aware search, I regard this as a scope clarification rather than a defect requiring revision.The grid target remains unproved by this note.1. The quadratic capacity bound is validLet  $R$  be the number of residual edges, counting parallel edges separately, and take the supplied formula\n$$K=\\sum_C\\min(v_C,e_C)$$\nas the dependency.For a connected component containing a cycle, including a two-edge parallel cycle, its rank is\n$$k=v_C=h+w,$$\nwhere  $h,w$  are its bipartition sizes. Unique intersection of a horizontal and vertical original run, together with at most two residual units per tile, gives\n$$e_C\\le 2hw\n\\le \\left\\lfloor\\frac{(h+w)^2}{2}\\right\\rfloor\n=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{k^2}{2}\\right\\rfloor.$$\nThis correctly uses the physical multiplicity-two restriction.Let  $t$  be the sum of the ranks of tree components and  $u=\\sum_i k_i$  the sum of the ranks of cyclic components. Tree components contribute exactly  $t$  edges; isolated vertices contribute neither rank nor edges.If there are cyclic components, then  $u\\ge2$ , and\n$$\\begin{aligned}\nR\n\u0026\\le t+\\sum_i\\left\\lfloor\\frac{k_i^2}{2}\\right\\rfloor\\\\\n\u0026\\le t+\\left\\lfloor\\frac{\\sum_i k_i^2}{2}\\right\\rfloor\\\\\n\u0026\\le t+\\left\\lfloor\\frac{u^2}{2}\\right\\rfloor\\\\\n\u0026\\le \\left\\lfloor\\frac{(t+u)^2}{2}\\right\\rfloor.\n\\end{aligned}$$\nThe last step follows from\n$$(t+u)^2-u^2-2t=t^2+2t(u-1)\\ge0$$\nand the integrality of  $t$ . There is no floor-function gap.If there are no cyclic components, then  $R=K$ . Consequently: $K=0$  implies  $R=0$ . $K=1$  implies  $R=1$ , because a cyclic component would already contribute rank at least two.For  $K\\ge2$ , the forest case satisfies  $R=K\\le\\lfloor K^2/2\\rfloor$ .Thus the exceptional small cases and the general bound are all correct.SharpnessThe blank rectangle with\n$$h=\\lfloor K/2\\rfloor,\\qquad w=\\lceil K/2\\rceil$$\ntile rows and columns has a doubled complete bipartite incidence graph. Its edge count is\n$$R=2hw=\\left\\lfloor K^2/2\\right\\rfloor,$$\nand its rank is  $h+w=K$ . At  $K=2$ , the two parallel edges correctly form a cyclic component.This establishes sharpness over the class of suitably sized even rectangles. It does not assert attainability for every  $K$  inside any one prescribed rectangle, or simultaneous attainability with a prescribed receiver charge. The note’s subsequent discussion respects that distinction.The multiplicity restriction is essential: if three parallel edges were permitted, a two-vertex component could have  $K=2$ ,  $R=3$ , invalidating the claimed small-rank bound. The supplied general incidence-matching theorem alone would therefore be insufficient; the note correctly supplies the additional physical restriction.2. Four is a necessary residual-charge thresholdUnder the assumed identity\n$$\\sigma=R-q,$$\ncertificate failure and target survival give exactly\n$$K\u003cq\\le R.$$\nFor  $K=0,1,2$ , Lemma 1 gives  $R\\le K$ , contradicting this chain. Therefore\n$$K\\ge3.$$\nBecause  $q$  is integral,\n$$q\\ge K+1\\ge4,\n\\qquad\nR\\ge q\\ge4.$$\nThis proves Lemma 2 without an omitted boundary case. Negative or zero residual charge cannot cause difficulty, since  $q\u003eK\\ge0$  already excludes it.Integrality is implicit in reserving distinct actual units and placing one edge per residual unit. For complete formal clarity, the reservation declaration could be written\n$$a_Q\\in\\{0,1,\\ldots,d_Q\\}.$$\nThis is an optional clarification, not a substantive gap. Also, the assumed identity itself ensures that  $\\rho$  is integral:  $D$  and\n$$\\sigma=mn/2-|T|-|M|$$\nare integers.The precise logical statement is\n$$\\bigl(q\u003eK\\ \\text{and}\\ \\sigma\\ge0\\bigr)\\Longrightarrow q\\ge4.$$\nIts stated equivalent for  $q\\le3$  is valid. Neither implication establishes  $\\sigma\\ge0$  for arbitrary configurations.3. The charge-four classification is complete at the residual graph levelAssume\n$$q=4,\\qquad K\u003cq,\\qquad \\sigma\\ge0.$$\nLemma 2 gives  $K\\ge3$ , so  $K=3$ . Then\n$$4\\le R\\le\\left\\lfloor\\frac{9}{2}\\right\\rfloor=4.$$\nHence\n$$\\boxed{K=3,\\qquad R=4,\\qquad \\sigma=0.}$$\nThe component argument is exhaustive.If there were at least two positive-rank components, their ranks would partition three as either  $2+1$  or  $1+1+1$ . Rank-one components have one edge, and rank-two components have at most two edges. Either partition therefore permits at most three edges, contradicting  $R=4$ .There is exactly one positive-rank component. It cannot be a tree, since then its edge count would equal its rank, namely three. It is therefore cyclic, with\n$$v_C=3,\\qquad e_C=4.$$\nA connected bipartite graph on three vertices has bipartition sizes  $1$  and  $2$ . There are only two possible run pairs. Since each has multiplicity at most two, four edges force both pairs to be doubled.Thus, apart from isolated vertices, the unique graph is a doubled  $K_{1,2}$ . No additional disconnected or parallel-edge case is missing.There is also an immediate converse at this level: if  $q=4$  and the residual graph has precisely this form, then  $K=3$ ,  $R=4$ , and the assumed identity gives  $\\sigma=0$ . Accordingly, this is a complete characterization within the stated  $q=4$  setting. It does not construct a physical configuration carrying that charge.Translation back to tilesThe physical conclusions follow correctly:Each of the two occupied run pairs comes from exactly one tile, because a horizontal and vertical original run intersect in at most one tile.Each such tile has two residual units.Consequently,\n$$2=r_Q=d_Q-a_Q\\le d_Q\\le2$$\nforces\n$$r_Q=d_Q=2,\\qquad a_Q=0.$$\nThe identity  $d_Q=2-|T\\cap Q|$  gives  $T\\cap Q=\\varnothing$ , and membership in  $Z$  excludes matching endpoints.The common graph vertex gives a shared original horizontal or vertical run; the other two vertices give distinct perpendicular runs.The note correctly declines to infer adjacency. Zero-residual tiles may lie between the two support tiles while remaining part of the same original  $Z$ -run. Keeping the original runs after reservation is therefore material to the statement.4. Reservations: exactly what is forcedAt residual charge four, the conclusions also give\n$$\\boxed{\\rho=D=B+4.}$$\nIndeed,\n$$\\rho=q+B=4+B,\\qquad D=R+B=4+B.$$\nThis identifies the important scope boundary:Quantity or propertyForced at residual charge four? $R=4$ ,  $K=3$ ,  $\\sigma=0$ YesExactly two positive-residual tilesYesNo reservation on those two tilesYes $B=0$  globallyNoOriginal charge  $\\rho=4$ Only when  $B=0$ Original capacity  $D=4$ Only when  $B=0$ Absence of available corner donorsNot established by these lemmasAdjacency of the two support tilesNot establishedFor every other  $Z$ -tile, the exact conclusion is\n$$r_Q=0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a_Q=d_Q.$$\nSuch a tile may have positive original capacity that has been fully reserved. “Every other tile has zero residual capacity” therefore does not imply “every other tile has zero original capacity.”The failure condition is also relative to the chosen reservation scheme. Failure for one scheme does not establish failure for every admissible scheme.There is a useful conditional observation concerning donors. If original charge is independently fixed at  $\\rho=4$ , the target survives, and an admissible reservation with  $B\\ge1$  exists, then\n$$q=4-B\\le3.$$\nLemma 2 then rules out  $q\u003eK$  for that scheme. Thus failure for every admissible reservation scheme, at original charge four, would exclude any admissible positive reservation. Turning that into “no old corner donors” additionally requires a stated connection between such donors and admissible"},"finish_reason":null}]}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl_6235d011-89c7-48ed-9fd6-f9d5ba2a93f0","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1792436456,"model":"oracle/chrono-chatgpt-pro-pool","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":" positive reservations. That connection is not proved in this note.5. Physical realization and the final search paragraphThe blank  $2\\times4$  vertex rectangle realizes the doubled-star resource graph. With no reservations, it has  $\\rho=q=0$ , so it does not supply the charge-four antecedent. The note expressly acknowledges this and makes no unsupported physical-realization claim.Likewise, the classification determines the residual incidence structure and its two support tiles. It does not determine the surrounding matching, receiver components, run lengths, available donor choices, or a physically frozen charge-four realization.I read the final paragraph’s “first separating search can require” as permission to choose a restricted initial search. On that reading it is sound. For greater precision, I recommend replacing its opening with:A restricted first search may impose  $B=0$ , original charge  $\\rho=4$ , and the absence of old corner donors as additional search restrictions. Any target-preserving certificate failure in this subfamily must have  $D=R=4$ ,  $K=3$ ,  $\\sigma=0$ , and the residual support described in Lemma 3. More generally, residual-charge-four configurations may have  $\\rho=D=4+B$ . The exhaustive classification concerns their residual support.Here “outside capacity” should be replaced by  $D$  or  $R$ , according to the intended meaning. This wording preserves the valid theorem and makes explicit that the proposed first search need not cover every configuration with residual charge four.   low-charge-rank-barrier.mdDocumentOpen file"},"finish_reason":null}]}

